---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';
const updated = new Date(site.termsUpdated).toLocaleDateString('en-AU', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' });
const law = site.legalState ? `the laws of ${site.legalState}, Australia` : 'the laws in force in Australia';
const courts = site.legalState ? `the courts of ${site.legalState}` : 'the courts of Australia';
// BOILERPLATE: a sensible starting point for a small Australian software business.
// It is not legal advice: have it reviewed if your apps take payments, hold
// accounts or are used in safety-critical work.
---
<Base title="Terms of service" description={`The terms that apply when you use the ${site.legalName} website, apps and software.`}>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Legal</span>
      <h1>Terms of service</h1>
      <p class="lead">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>
        These terms apply when you use this website, our apps, or software published by {site.legalName}
        ("Datavis", "we", "us"). By using them you agree to these terms. If you do not agree, please do not use them.
      </p>

      <h2 id="services">What these terms cover</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>This website</strong>: www.datavis.com.au and its documentation.</li>
        <li><strong>Our apps</strong>: apps we publish on the Apple App Store, Google Play and other stores.</li>
        <li><strong>Our software</strong>: packages, libraries and tools, both open source and commercial.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Some products come with their own terms. Where they do, those terms apply to that product as well as these:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Open source projects</strong> are licensed under the licence in their GitHub repository (for
          example zlib or Apache 2.0). That licence sets out what you may do with the code.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Commercial products</strong>, such as PRO packages and Drill and Blast software, are supplied
          under a separate licence agreement. If it conflicts with these terms, the licence agreement applies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>App store terms.</strong> When you download an app, the store's own terms also apply: the
          <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/internet-services/itunes/dev/stdeula/" rel="noopener">Apple Licensed Application End User Licence Agreement</a>
          for the App Store, and the
          <a href="https://play.google.com/about/play-terms/" rel="noopener">Google Play Terms of Service</a> for Google Play.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="licence">Using our apps</h2>
      <p>
        We give you a personal, non-exclusive, non-transferable licence to install and use our apps on devices
        you own or control, for your own personal or business use. You must not:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>copy, modify, reverse engineer or resell an app, except where the law or an open source licence allows it;</li>
        <li>use an app in a way that breaks the law or harms other people;</li>
        <li>try to interfere with or gain unauthorised access to our services or systems.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We may update an app to fix problems or add features, and we may stop offering an app. Where an app
        has been paid for, the store's refund terms and the Australian Consumer Law still apply.
      </p>

      <h2 id="purchases">Purchases</h2>
      <p>
        Purchases made in our apps are processed by the Apple App Store or Google Play, under their payment and
        refund terms. Commercial licences bought directly from us are covered by the invoice and licence agreement
        we send you.
      </p>

      <h2 id="safety">Safe and appropriate use</h2>
      <p>
        Some of our apps and tools relate to motorsport, robotics and mining. They are for practice, planning,
        simulation and information:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Motorsport apps, such as Drag Tree, are training and data tools. They are not official race timing
          or safety equipment. Never use an app while driving on a public road, and always follow the rules of
          your track or event.
        </li>
        <li>
          Engineering, robotics and mining software supports qualified people. It does not replace professional
          judgement, site procedures or the safety laws that apply to your work. Check results before you act on them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="content">Your content</h2>
      <p>
        Anything you create or record with our apps stays yours. Where you send us something, such as a support
        email or a bug report, you allow us to use it to help you and to improve our products. Our
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> explains how we handle personal information.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ip">Our intellectual property</h2>
      <p>
        The content of this website and our apps, and the Datavis name and logos, belong to {site.legalName} unless
        stated otherwise. All rights are reserved, apart from what an open source licence grants for that project.
        Other names and trademarks belong to their owners.
      </p>

      <h2 id="acl">Your rights under Australian Consumer Law</h2>
      <p>
        Our products come with guarantees that cannot be excluded under the Australian Consumer Law. Nothing in
        these terms excludes, restricts or changes those rights, or any other right that cannot lawfully be excluded.
      </p>

      <h2 id="liability">Limits on our liability</h2>
      <p>
        To the extent the law allows:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>we provide this website, our free apps and our open source software "as is", without warranties of any kind;</li>
        <li>we are not liable for indirect or consequential loss, such as lost profits, lost data or lost race results;</li>
        <li>
          where a guarantee under the Australian Consumer Law has not been met and the law lets us limit our
          liability, our liability is limited to supplying the product or service again, or paying the cost of
          having it supplied again.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="links">Links to other sites</h2>
      <p>
        This website links to other sites, such as GitHub, MuJoCo and the app stores. We are not responsible for
        their content or how they handle your information.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ending">Ending your use</h2>
      <p>
        You can stop using our apps at any time by uninstalling them. We may suspend access if you use our
        services in breach of these terms.
      </p>

      <h2 id="changes">Changes to these terms</h2>
      <p>
        We may update these terms, for example when we release a new app or feature. We will change the date at
        the top of this page. If you keep using our website, apps or software after a change, the updated terms apply.
      </p>

      <h2 id="law">Governing law</h2>
      <p>
        These terms are governed by {law}. You and we submit to the non-exclusive jurisdiction of {courts}.
        If any part of these terms is found to be unenforceable, the rest still applies.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        {site.legalName}{site.abn && <> (ABN {site.abn})</>}, {site.country}. Questions about these terms:
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email.general}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Terms of service')}`}>{site.email.general}</a>.
        For help with an app, see <a href="/support/">Support</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
